 ## Overview
-This method accelerates volumetric path tracing by learning a geometric approximation of random light paths in homogeneous volumes to determine absorption and direct lighting contributions, bypassing lengthy internal scattering processes by summarizing many scattering events into a single sphere-tracing step [S1]. It differs from shell tracing by using neural networks to model statistical distributions rather than discretized transition probability arrays, and from analytic models by avoiding assumptions of planar surfaces or isotropic transport [S1]. It builds on [Anisotropic Scattering via CVAE Sampling](19952-anisotropic-scattering-via-cvae-sampling.md) by extending the CVAE approach to jointly model the number of scattering events, absorption, exit position, and direction within a sphere.
+This method accelerates volumetric path tracing by learning a geometric approximation of random light paths in homogeneous volumes to determine absorption and direct lighting contributions, bypassing lengthy internal scattering processes by summarizing many scattering events into a single sphere-tracing step [S1]. It differs from shell tracing by using neural networks to model statistical distributions rather than discretized transition probability arrays, and from analytic models by avoiding assumptions of planar surfaces or isotropic transport [S1]. It builds on [Anisotropic Scattering via CVAE Sampling](19952-anisotropic-scattering-via-cvae-sampling.md) by extending the CVAE approach to jointly model the number of scattering events, absorption, exit position, and direction within a sphere [S1].
 
